// ============================================================================
// main.bx — the entry point of the Burxt compiler, written in Burxt.
//
// **This file was called `stage1.bx` until v0.0.215, and the rename is the point.**
// Every one of its neighbours is named for what it DOES — `parser.bx`, `check.bx`,
// `emit.bx` — and then the entry point was named for its position in a bootstrap
// sequence. "Stage 1" answers *which step of building something else is this*, which is
// a question only whoever built it ever asks. A maintainer opening this directory asks
// *where does the program start*, and the answer is spelled `main` in every language
// there is — including in `src/rust-compiler/main.rs`, sitting in the other compiler at
// the same place doing the same job.
//
// It is also no longer TRUE in the way the name implied. A stage is a step toward a
// destination you have not reached yet; Burxt compiles Burxt to a byte-identical
// fixpoint, so this is not on the way to a compiler, it IS one. The name outlived what
// it described — the same rot that `spec/M4-SELF-HOSTING.md` §3b was corrected for in
// this same version, and worth recording twice because it happened twice.
//
// (**"stage-0" and "stage-1" survive as PROSE**, in the specs, where the subject really
// is the bootstrap: which compiler built which, and why two of them must agree. That is
// a defined term doing work in a sentence about history. A filename is not a sentence.)
//
// ---- the two compilers, side by side ---------------------------------------------
//
//   src/burxt-compiler/            src/rust-compiler/
//   main.bx     the entry point    main.rs      the entry point and CLI
//   types.bx    shapes, lexer      ast.rs + lexer.rs
//   parser.bx   tokens in, arena AST out        parser.rs
//   check.bx    scales, regions, purity, contracts, exhaustiveness    typeck.rs
//   emit.bx     textual LLVM IR + the runtime   codegen.rs (LLVM's C API, via inkwell)
//               — and nothing here yet for     diag.rs · lsp.rs · review.rs ·
//                 the tooling                    schema.rs · json.rs
//
// The last row is the honest asymmetry and the only one: the LANGUAGE is covered on both
// sides, the TOOLING is not. See `spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md` §A0.
//
// The program is this file; the compiler is the modules it uses. It was one file of
// nearly six thousand lines until v0.0.82, because Burxt had no module system — and
// splitting it is the acceptance test for having one. If the compiler cannot be
// split, modules are not done.
//
//   types.bx    the shapes, and the lexer
//   parser.bx   tokens in, arena AST out
//   check.bx    the rules: scales, regions, purity, contracts, exhaustiveness
//   modules.bx  `use "path";`, resolved before lexing
//   emit.bx     textual LLVM IR, and the runtime emitted with it
//
// Build and run it with:
//
//     burxt build src/burxt-compiler/main.bx -o /tmp/burxt-in-burxt
//     /tmp/burxt-in-burxt <program.bx> [out.ll]
//
// Given a second argument it emits LLVM IR for the program; given one it lexes,
// parses and checks it, and reports what it found.
// ============================================================================

use "types.bx";
use "parser.bx";
use "check.bx";
use "modules.bx";
use "emit.bx";
use "diag.bx";

// The shell, for `build` and `run`. Those two have to invoke `llc` and `cc`, and until
// v0.0.219 the Burxt compiler could not: it emitted IR to a file and a human ran the rest
// by hand. That is the difference between an IR emitter and a compiler, and it is why this
// one line was the centre of the parity gate rather than the CLI parsing around it.
//
// `system` was already declared in `lib/os.bx` and already in use, so this needed measuring
// rather than designing — the same lesson `getchar` taught one version earlier. The compiler
// modules do not depend on `lib/`, so the declaration is repeated here rather than imported.
external function system(command: String) -> CInt touches commands;

// ---- the pipeline, once ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **A `Unit` cannot be RETURNED from a function, and that is not a bug.** The first version of
// this file factored the arena out into a maker function, and the compiler refused it by name:
//
//     function `fresh_unit` cannot return Unit, because its storage lives in a region and
//     would not outlive it. Fill an array the caller owns, or return a scalar summary.
//
// That is the region model's stated cost — `DESIGN.md` calls the granularity "the honest limit
// of this model, accepted deliberately" — arriving as a sentence at the moment it applies,
// which is the entire argument for the language. So the arena is built where it is used, and
// `drive` owns one for the length of one compilation.
//
// It has a consequence worth flagging for `review.bx`, which needs TWO units to compare two
// files: they cannot be handed to it by a factory. Both must be local to whatever function
// does the comparing, or the comparison runs twice and keeps a scalar summary between passes.

// The IR module: the runtime's globals, then everything the emitter built.
//
// The globals are here rather than in `emit.bx` because they are the same for every program
// — a preamble, not a product of the source. Four of them carry a length header because they
// are Burxt Strings that `to_string` can answer with; the `fmt_*` ones are C format strings
// and must not.
function module_text(mutable unit: Unit) -> String {
return "; LLVM IR written by the Burxt compiler, in Burxt.\n"
    + "@burxt.fmt_int = private unnamed_addr constant [5 x i8] c\"%lld\\00\"\n"
    + "@burxt.fmt_str = private unnamed_addr constant [3 x i8] c\"%s\\00\"\n"
    + "@burxt.fmt_nl = private unnamed_addr constant [2 x i8] c\"\\0A\\00\"\n"
    // These four are Burxt STRINGS, not printf formats, so they carry a length
    // header like every other String — `to_string(true)` answers one of them, and
    // `len` of it would otherwise read whatever the linker placed before the bytes.
    // The `fmt_*` globals above are C format strings and need none.
    + "@burxt.string_true = private unnamed_addr constant \{ i64, [5 x i8] \} "
    + "\{ i64 4, [5 x i8] c\"true\\00\" \}\n"
    + "@burxt.string_false = private unnamed_addr constant \{ i64, [6 x i8] \} "
    + "\{ i64 5, [6 x i8] c\"false\\00\" \}\n"
    + "@burxt.string_minus = private unnamed_addr constant \{ i64, [2 x i8] \} "
    + "\{ i64 1, [2 x i8] c\"-\\00\" \}\n"
    + "@burxt.string_empty = private unnamed_addr constant \{ i64, [1 x i8] \} "
    + "\{ i64 0, [1 x i8] c\"\\00\" \}\n"
    + "@burxt.overflow_msg = private unnamed_addr constant [42 x i8] "
    + "c\"burxt runtime error: arithmetic overflow\\0A\\00\"\n"
    + "@burxt.region_msg = private unnamed_addr constant [87 x i8] "
    + "c\"burxt runtime error: region memory exhausted — this build "
    + "reserves 1 GB per process\\0A\\00\"\n"
    + "@burxt.divzero_msg = private unnamed_addr constant [39 x i8] "
    + "c\"burxt runtime error: division by zero\\0A\\00\"\n"
    + "@burxt.bounds_msg = private unnamed_addr constant [76 x i8] "
    + "c\"burxt runtime error: index %lld is outside an array of %lld"
    + " (at byte %lld)\\0A\\00\"\n"
    + unit.globals + "\n" + unit.finish();
}

// Everything a program needs to become IR, in one place: load it with what it uses, lex,
// parse, check, and emit if asked.
//
// `out_ll` empty means check only. `loud` is 1 for the legacy report — the token counts and
// per-phase work that `spec/M9-PERFORMANCE.md` reads, and that three runner invariants parse
// — and 0 for the subcommands, which must be quiet on success the way every other compiler
// is. Both paths share this function so the two cannot drift, which is the whole reason it
// is a function and not two copies.
//
// Answers the number of problems found: 0 means it worked.
function drive(path: String, out_ll: String, loud: Int) -> Int touches files {
    // The program AND everything it uses, in one buffer. The same design stage-0 uses, for
    // the same reason: a Span stays a byte range, and only a renderer needs to know which
    // file an offset fell in. See spec/M6-MODULES.md §1.5.
    let src: String = load_program(path);
    let mutable unit: Unit = Unit { tokens: [], keywords: [], nodes: [], children: [], nested_children: [], items: [], bindings: [], functions: [], stack: [], methods: [], types: [], params_in_scope: [], generics: [], type_parameters: [], type_param_bounds: [], pending_bounds: [], current_generic: 0 - 1, current_method: 0 - 1, probing: 0, probe_changes: 0, type_bindings: [], measure_clause: 0 - 1, measure_slot: 0, measure_parameters: 0 - 1, measure_owner: "", old_nodes: [], old_slots: [], ensures_start: 0, ensures_count: 0, ensures_owner: "", bracket_clauses: [], clause_subjects: [], it_token: 0 - 1, it_is_result: 0, used_it: 0, it_seen: 0, result_type_node: 0 - 1, result_slot: 0 - 1, returns_node: 0 - 1, walked_types: [], type_binding_floor: 0, allowed_effects: 63, effects_owner: "", function_buckets: [], function_chain: [], instances: [], instance_arguments: [], impls: [], typed: [], region_depth: 0, in_caller_region: 0, in_pure: 0, loop_depth: 0, in_contract: 0, in_ensures: 0, current_function: -1, chunks: [], pending: "", globals: "", sret: "", sret_cells: 0, function_entry: "", body_chunks: [], body_pending: "", next_register: 0, next_slot: 0, next_label: 0, slots: [], strings: [], declared: [], loop_condition_labels: [], loop_end_labels: [], in_function: 0, depth: 0, problems: 0, position: 0, token_end: 0, no_record_literal: 0, in_multiply: 0, print_to_stderr: 0, int_type_node: 0 - 1, errors: 0, type_of_calls: 0, find_binding_calls: 0, find_function_calls: 0, show_type_calls: 0, expr_allocates_calls: 0, type_at_calls: 0 };
    let installed: Int = unit.install_keywords();
    let total: Int = unit.run(src);

    if loud == 1 {
        print("lexed " + path + ": " + to_string(total) + " tokens, "
              + to_string(installed) + " keywords known");
        print("  identifiers: " + to_string(unit.count_of(1)));
        print("  decimals:    " + to_string(unit.count_of(3)));
        print("  strings:     " + to_string(unit.count_of(4)));
        print("  errors:      " + to_string(unit.errors));
        print("first tokens:");
        let shown: Int = unit.report(src, 8);
    }
    if unit.errors > 0 && loud == 0 {
        return unit.errors;
    }

    let mutable items: Int = 0;
    while !unit.done() {
        let item: Int = unit.parse_item(src);
        if item < 0 {
            break;
        }
        push(unit.items, item);
        items += 1;
    }
    if loud == 1 {
        print("parsed " + to_string(items) + " items and statements into "
              + to_string(len(unit.nodes)) + " nodes, "
              + to_string(len(unit.children)) + " child slots");
        print("  parse errors: " + to_string(unit.errors));
    }
    if unit.errors > 0 {
        return unit.errors;
    }

    let problems: Int = unit.check(src);
    if loud == 1 {
        print("checked " + to_string(len(unit.functions)) + " functions and "
              + to_string(len(unit.types)) + " types");
        print("  type errors: " + to_string(problems));
        // M9: what the checker actually did, per run. A number beside a phase is the only
        // thing that turns "the checker is slow" into a place to look.
        print("  work: " + to_string(len(unit.nodes)) + " nodes, type_of "
              + to_string(unit.type_of_calls) + ", find_binding " + to_string(unit.find_binding_calls)
              + " over " + to_string(len(unit.bindings)) + " bindings, find_function "
              + to_string(unit.find_function_calls) + " over " + to_string(len(unit.functions))
              + " functions");
    }
    if problems > 0 {
        return problems;
    }
    if len(out_ll) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }

    let emitted: Int = unit.emit_module(src);
    if unit.problems > 0 {
        if loud == 1 {
            print("  backend refusals: " + to_string(unit.problems));
        } else {
            print_error("burxt: the backend refused " + to_string(unit.problems)
                        + " construct(s) in " + path);
        }
        return unit.problems;
    }
    let text: String = module_text(unit);
    let wrote: Int = write_file(out_ll, text);
    if loud == 1 {
        print("wrote " + out_ll + ", " + to_string(len(text)) + " bytes of IR");
    }
    return 0;
}

// ---- the shape of a command line -------------------------------------------------------

// The name a built program should get when nobody said: `pay.bx` in any directory becomes
// `./pay`, which is what `burxt build` in Rust does.
//
// Written out rather than derived from the path by a rule the caller cannot see, because a
// DERIVED name is a reference no sweep can find — `spec/A7.0-NAMING.md` §9 was written after
// exactly that cost three broken tests during the `stage1.bx` rename. Here the derivation is
// unavoidable (it is the default), so it lives in one named function instead of inline.
function default_output_name(path: String) -> String {
    let mutable start: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(path) {
        if byte_at(path, i) == 47 {
            start = i + 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    let mutable stop: Int = len(path);
    let mutable j: Int = start;
    while j < len(path) {
        if byte_at(path, j) == 46 {
            stop = j;
        }
        j += 1;
    }
    return "./" + substring(path, start, stop - start);
}

// Is this word a subcommand, or the path of a program?
//
// **This is the compatibility hinge and it is worth stating plainly.** Three runner
// invariants run `stage1 <file.bx>` with no subcommand and read the legacy report — one of
// them parses `type errors:` out of it. So the dispatch below may only fire on a word it
// RECOGNISES; anything else is a path and behaves exactly as it did before. A dispatcher that
// assumed argument 1 was always a command would have broken all three, and the failure would
// have looked like a checker regression rather than a CLI change.
function is_subcommand(word: String) -> Bool {
    if word == "check" || word == "build" || word == "run" || word == "emit-ir" {
        return true;
    }
    if word == "--help" || word == "-h" || word == "help" || word == "--version" || word == "-V" {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

function usage() -> Int {
    print("burxt 0.0.219 — the Burxt compiler, written in Burxt");
    print("usage:");
    print("  burxt check   <file.bx>                  parse and typecheck only");
    print("  burxt build   <file.bx> [-o <path>]      compile to a native executable");
    print("  burxt run     <file.bx>                  compile then run");
    print("  burxt emit-ir <file.bx>                  print LLVM IR");
    print("  burxt <file.bx> [out.ll]                 the phase report, and IR if asked");
    print("");
    print("Not here yet, and named rather than silently missing:");
    print("  layout, explain memory, review, mcp-schema, lsp, --json, --target");
    print("They exist in the Rust build. spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md THE GATE tracks the rest.");
    return 0;
}

// ---- the command line ------------------------------------------------------------------

let mutable command: String = "";
if argument_count() > 1 {
    command = argument(1);
}

if command == "--version" || command == "-V" {
    print("burxt 0.0.219 — the Burxt compiler, written in Burxt");
    exit(0);
}

if command == "--help" || command == "-h" || command == "help" {
    let helped: Int = usage();
    exit(0);
}

if is_subcommand(command) && argument_count() < 3 {
    print_error("burxt " + command + ": needs a file");
    exit(2);
}

if command == "check" {
    let problems: Int = drive(argument(2), "", 0);
    if problems == 0 {
        print(argument(2) + ": no errors");
        exit(0);
    }
    exit(1);
}

if command == "emit-ir" {
    // Through a file and then read back, because `print` is the only way out and the IR is
    // one string. An honest cost, and the reason it is acceptable: `build` needs the file on
    // disk anyway, so nothing is written that was not needed.
    let scratch: String = "/tmp/burxt-emit-ir.ll";
    let problems: Int = drive(argument(2), scratch, 0);
    if problems > 0 {
        exit(1);
    }
    print(read_file(scratch));
    exit(0);
}

if command == "build" || command == "run" {
    let source: String = argument(2);
    let mutable output: String = default_output_name(source);
    if argument_count() > 4 {
        if argument(3) == "-o" {
            output = argument(4);
        }
    }
    let ll: String = output + ".ll";
    let object: String = output + ".o";
    let problems: Int = drive(source, ll, 0);
    if problems > 0 {
        exit(1);
    }
    // llc then cc, the same two steps the Rust build makes — it just makes them through
    // LLVM's C API instead of through a shell. The IR is textual either way, which is what
    // makes the two paths comparable at all.
    let assembled: Int = system("/usr/lib/llvm-18/bin/llc -relocation-model=pic -filetype=obj -o "
                                + object + " " + ll);
    if assembled != 0 {
        print_error("burxt: llc could not assemble " + ll);
        exit(1);
    }
    let linked: Int = system("cc -o " + output + " " + object);
    if linked != 0 {
        print_error("burxt: cc could not link " + object);
        exit(1);
    }
    if command == "build" {
        print("compiled " + source + " -> " + output);
        exit(0);
    }
    // `run` hands the program's own exit status back to the shell, because a test suite
    // written in Burxt reports failure that way — `lib/test.bx` ends with `exit(test_end(t))`
    // and it would be pointless if the runner swallowed it.
    let ran: Int = system(output);
    exit(divide_floor(ran, 256));
}

// ---- no subcommand: the legacy report, unchanged ---------------------------------------
//
// Three runner invariants depend on every line of this, including the exact text
// `  type errors: N`. It is not a debug mode left in by accident: it is what
// `spec/M9-PERFORMANCE.md` reads to attribute a slowdown to a phase, and what the
// differential tests parse to compare the two checkers.

let mutable path: String = "examples/tour.bx";
if argument_count() > 1 {
    path = argument(1);
}
let mutable out_ll: String = "";
if argument_count() > 2 {
    out_ll = argument(2);
}
let found: Int = drive(path, out_ll, 1);
